# Secrets. The scaffolder's own next-steps say `cp .env.example .env`, and it
# ships no .gitignore — so this file is the only thing standing between a
# `git add -A` and a committed APP_SECRET_KEY / JWT_SECRET /
# SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local overnight-run overrides (deploy host lives here, not in the tracked
# .overnight.conf).
.overnight.local.conf

# Which 1Password item holds your dev admin login, for `make dev-up`. Same
# arrangement as .overnight.local.conf above: the item's title is a hostname,
# so it is named here and nowhere tracked. See scripts/dev-admin.sh.
.devstack.local.conf

# Log shipping. The overlay compose reads is a copy of the tracked
# compose.loki.yaml.example, and it stays untracked for the same reason as the
# two files above: it presumes one site's Loki, one tenant's label set, and a
# credential that lives in .env. Copy the example, do not commit the copy.
compose.loki.yaml

# Node
node_modules/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Playwright artefacts. `test-results/` holds the strip screenshots the
# specs write on every run, plus failure screenshots and traces — a failed
# run's screenshot is a full page, so it can carry a session's email
# address, and none of it belongs in the history. The one image that IS
# committed is `docs/ops/img/resolution-strip.png`, checked by eye first.
frontend/test-results/
frontend/playwright-report/
frontend/blob-report/
frontend/.playwright/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# OS
.DS_Store

# Extracted TLS material (a copy of the old service.s ACME cert).
certs/

# This stack's own ACME store, after the hand-over. It holds the account
# private key and every issued certificate's private key — the one file in
# the deployment whose loss means re-issuing and whose disclosure means
# somebody else can speak as the service.
letsencrypt/

# Conductor's per-workspace scratch directory: agent notes, todo lists,
# whatever is mid-thought. It was ignored through `.git/info/exclude`, which
# is machine-local — so it protected this checkout and nobody else's. Tracked
# here instead now that the repository is public and clones are other people's.
.context/

# --- What a deploy host writes into its own checkout (#76) -----------------
#
# `certs/` and `letsencrypt/` above cover the two directories this stack
# creates by itself. Everything below covers the loose files an *operator*
# leaves in the checkout while running the cutover runbook — the class that
# a hurried `git add -A` during a cutover window turns into a commit.
#
# The incident: a deploy directory held a `.admin-credential` file, mode
# 0600, untracked and matched by no pattern here, so `git status` listed it
# as `??`. It was mitigated on that host by appending it to
# `.git/info/exclude`, which is local to one checkout and travels nowhere.
#
# **Nothing in this repository writes that file, and nothing needs to.**
# The issue proposed "show it once instead of persisting it" as the better
# fix; there was no writer to change. Checked three ways rather than
# assumed: a repo-wide grep for the name returns zero hits; `make
# seed-admin` passes `--email/--password` to `app.scripts.seed_admin` and
# that module — atrium's, read in the upstream checkout — contains no
# `open(`, no `write`, no `Path(`, and prints its result; and
# `scripts/dev-admin.sh` reads the login out of 1Password and writes no
# file at all — it only *tells* the operator to create
# `.devstack.local.conf`, which is ignored above. The "shown once"
# discipline the device secrets follow is what the code already does. The
# file was made by hand.
#
# So nothing below persists because it *must*. Two entries are the
# exception and carry their reason:
#
#  * `certs/` and `letsencrypt/` (above) hold TLS material that has to
#    exist on disk for the proxy to read, and are covered as directories.
#  * `*.db` / `*.db-wal` / `*.db-shm` — `scripts/copy-legacy-db.sh` has to
#    land the legacy copy somewhere the operator chooses, and the runbook
#    keeps it deliberately (§ 8.2, indefinitely, off the host). It has to
#    persist; it must not be stageable.
#
# Everything else is shaped as a *family* rather than as one filename,
# because the leak that has already happened is the one you cannot have
# again: a `.gitignore` line matching exactly `.admin-credential` stops
# matching the day someone types `admin-cred.txt`.
#
# Two things to know before widening this section further. Word-shaped
# patterns are root-anchored on purpose — `frontend/src/api/credentials.ts`
# is real source and an unanchored `*credential*` would silently un-stage
# the next file like it; `git ls-files --cached --ignored --exclude-standard`
# is the one-line check that no tracked file has been swallowed, and it is
# empty here. And macOS checkouts default to `core.ignorecase=true`, so
# `git check-ignore` on a laptop cannot tell `/*credential*` from
# `/*Credential*` — both spellings are present because the deploy host is
# Linux, and `git -c core.ignorecase=false check-ignore -v` is the reading
# that answers for it.

# Credential drops. Root-anchored, for the reason above.
/*credential*
/*Credential*
/*secret*
/*Secret*
/secrets/
*.credential
*.credentials
.htpasswd
.netrc
.pgpass
.my.cnf

# Keys and certificates. `TLS_CERT_DIR` and `ACME_STORE_DIR` default to
# `./certs` and `./letsencrypt` (both covered above) but are overridable,
# so the material is only reliably caught by its extension. The `.tmp`
# forms are `scripts/extract-acme-cert.sh`'s atomic-write intermediates,
# which survive an interrupted extraction.
*.pem
*.pem.tmp
*.key
*.key.tmp
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
*.ppk
acme.json
id_rsa*
id_dsa*
id_ecdsa*
id_ed25519*
deploy_key*

# Copies of the legacy database and anything dumped out of MySQL. The
# SQLite copy `scripts/copy-legacy-db.sh` takes is written to a path the
# operator names, and it carries the fleet's bcrypt hashes and the Fernet
# ciphertext of the Route 53 credential (docs/ops/cutover.md § 8.2). The
# `-wal` / `-shm` siblings appear beside any WAL database that gets opened
# in WAL mode — § 3 of that runbook is about exactly that.
#
# These three lines are not hypothetical and were not suggested by the
# issue. Sweeping the untracked files of every real checkout of this repo
# on the workstation — 41 of them, 123 distinct paths — found a legacy
# `dyndns.db` and an `events.db`, each with its `-wal` and `-shm`, sitting
# untracked and unignored at mode 0644, with a `users` table carrying
# `password_hash` and `totp_secret` columns. Same defect as the issue's, in
# a different file family, on a different machine.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sql
*.sql.gz
*.dump
*.bak
/instance/

# Release bundles. `scripts/dev-release.sh` writes `dist/<tag>.bundle`,
# which `dist/` already covers; `scripts/deploy-dev.sh` is handed a path,
# and a bundle carries the whole history including anything in it.
*.bundle
.gate-venv/
.test-venv/

# Agent worktrees. `.claude/worktrees/` holds one checkout per agent run and
# reached 63 entries here, which is what made `make release` refuse — its
# dirty-tree guard counted untracked files. The guard was fixed to refuse only
# tracked changes, but the noise had no reason to exist either.
#
# Blanket, not `.claude/worktrees/`: nothing under `.claude/` is tracked today.
# If this repo ever wants to share `.claude/settings.json` or an agent
# definition, `git add -f` it, or add a `!.claude/settings.json` negation.
.claude/
